WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.400
-Welcome back to the show.
You look gorgeous.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.267
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:05.868
We have a lot to talk about.

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.167
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:09.267
I mean, we have a new "Downton"
movie this Friday.

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:10.701
-Yes.
-You know what?

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:15.234
You're a rare guest that isn't
on any social media of any sort.

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:16.634
-I couldn't find anything on
you.

00:00:16.634 --> 00:00:20.367
-Well, I do --
I do have one actually.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.868
I have one for my dog.

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:24.200
I'm one of --
I'm one of those people.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.167
-You're one of those people.
Okay, so -- yeah, yeah.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:27.534
-And then I stalk everyone.

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:30.901
-Yeah. Oh, so you do know what's
happening on social media?

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.934
-Yeah.
-Through the eyes of your dog?

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:34.501
-Yes, through the eyes
of my dog. Yeah.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.467
-And no -- does anyone know
that it's your dog?

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:37.701
Are you in the picture
with the dog?

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.434
-No. No one knows it's my dog.
-Wow.

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:40.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:41.868
Wow, interesting.

00:00:41.868 --> 00:00:43.501
Yeah, I'm not going to be asking
what the dog's name is, but --

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:46.167
but so, you post photos
of your dog.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.200
-I post photos of my dog

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.234
and then I follow lots
of interior design.

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:52.634
It's quite boring really.

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:54.634
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.000
-Interior designers? Why?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.534
-Because I like --
I just love interiors.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.567
I like looking at nice houses.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.634
And --
-Wow.

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:03.868
That's what you look at on
Instagram.

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:05.200
That's so interesting.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.501
-Yeah, I got out of control.

00:01:06.501 --> 00:01:09.601
I started with -- I think,
I don't know, sunsets?

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:12.300
I don't know what I followed.
I followed, like, food.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.467
-I mean, you can sit there
for ages, can't you?

00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:16.067
Just scrolling food.
-Yeah, you -- I do --

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.067
I do, actually.
-I go, "Everyone goes down

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.968
a rabbit hole."
-A rabbit hole, yeah.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:22.434
All right, well, the dog itself
doesn't know

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:24.367
that it's on Instagram?
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.234
-No.
-[ Laughs ] I was --

00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:29.033
I'm not very good
at doing those things.

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:31.534
-Yeah.
-He is.

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:33.901
-Yeah, I get that.
-I let him run his own account.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:37.534
-Last time we talked,
we spoke about music,

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:40.834
and you've been playing a lot
of guitar and piano.

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.834
-Yes.
-But you now have an EP out.

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:43.934
-Yes.
-You have music out.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:45.167
-Yes.
-I was listening to you

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:48.467
last night on Spotify.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.434
Michael and Michelle.

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:51.968
-You have a beautiful voice,
by the way.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.934
-Thank you. Thank you.
-Well, how did this band

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:55.200
come about?

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.334
-So, Michael C. Fox is also
in "Downton Abbey."

00:01:58.334 --> 00:01:59.434
-Yep.
-He plays Andy.

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:03.234
And we started jamming together
on set

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:05.033
and bringing our guitars
into work,

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:08.467
and our voices sounded great
together,

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:11.334
and then we started songwriting
together.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:13.634
So -- So, yeah.
And this is our first EP.

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:15.767
-I -- I love this.

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:17.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.100
You're actually playing a gig --
-Yes.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.367
-Your first gig, live gig?

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:23.667
-First gig is 6th of June,
in London. Yeah.

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:25.234
-Are you nervous?
-Yeah.

00:02:25.234 --> 00:02:28.067
Like, equally nervous
and excited.

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:29.234
-Yeah.
-At the same time.

00:02:29.234 --> 00:02:30.834
-What was your first concert
ever you went to?

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:31.901
Do you remember?

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:34.400
-Yeah, my first concert,
I was about 14,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.534
and it was called
"Masters of Music"

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:37.801
in Hyde Park.

00:02:37.801 --> 00:02:42.234
And I saw Alanis Morissette,
Bob Dylan, The Who,

00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:44.100
and Eric Clapton.
-What?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.601
-All in one, all in one.

00:02:45.601 --> 00:02:46.901
-That was your first concert?

00:02:46.901 --> 00:02:48.267
-Yes, it was amazing.

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.567
The bar was very, very high.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.767
-Wow.
Mine was "Weird Al" Yankovic.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:56.567
The bar was still high,
but yeah.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.033
Wow, that's fantastic.
-Yeah, it was incredible.

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:01.100
But we were so far back.
We got, you know,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.267
the really cheap, cheap tickets,
and she was like this.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:05.767
-That's great, having everyone.
-Alanis Morissette.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:07.734
-She was tiny. I was obsessed
with her when I was a teenager.

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:09.133
-Who wasn't?

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:11.701
-I used to braid my hair at
night and then bring it out

00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:13.634
in the morning so it kind of
looked like hers.

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:14.968
-Oh, is that right?
-Yes.

00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:16.634
-Oh, my gosh.
-She got to dress like --

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:19.567
-Before we get to
"Downton Abbey," I want to say

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:21.200
congratulations on
"Anatomy of a Scandal."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.534
-Thanks.
-It got great reviews.

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:24.334
It's out. You crushed it.
We love Sienna Miller.

00:03:24.334 --> 00:03:25.567
-Thank you.
-Who's awesome.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:26.767
-Yes.

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:32.067
-When you -- when you meet a fan
to take a picture

00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:34.033
or anything like that,
do you know immediately

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:36.868
if they're a "Downton" fan or
"Anatomy of a Scandal" fan?

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:39.767
-Sometimes "Downton" fans sort
of -- there's this kind of

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.267
deference.
It's like they curtsy.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:43.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:45.467
-It's like meeting royalty.
Yeah.

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:46.901
-Yeah.
-Of course.

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:49.968
-Yes, if it's about "Anatomy
of a Scandal," I think people

00:03:49.968 --> 00:03:52.133
are a bit more afraid of --
-Yeah.

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:53.868
-More afraid of me.
-The lawyer side of you.

00:03:53.868 --> 00:03:58.167
Yeah. This -- And this new film,
"Downton Abbey" --

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:00.534
this is called
"Downton Abbey: A New Era."

00:04:00.534 --> 00:04:03.067
-Yes.
-The first "Downton Abbey"

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.834
was when -- 2010, I think?
-'11. Yeah.

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:09.234
Yeah, we started in 2010,
so it's been 12 years.

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:11.033
-Did you -- did you have any
idea that you would be

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:12.534
playing your character
for 12 years?

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:14.534
-No, we would have never
imagined.

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:16.234
I mean, I can't believe
I'm still sitting here

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:17.400
talking about it. [ Laughs ]

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.334
-Yeah, but it's great.
-It's amazing. Yeah.

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:21.334
-But then you then put the movie
out and you're like,

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:22.434
"Well, let's hope it's --
-Yes.

00:04:22.434 --> 00:04:24.167
-And it was a giant hit.
-Yes.

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:27.033
And that's -- you know, it's
about the appetite out there,

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.434
and the audience really, you
know, loved the first film.

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:31.267
It made sense to do another one.
-Yeah.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:34.334
-And -- and this film,
it's fantastic.

00:04:34.334 --> 00:04:37.200
There's, like, two parallel
storylines going on.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.701
One is some of the characters
get to go off

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:44.567
to the South of France and
discover Lady Violet's past,

00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.000
something about her past.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.067
And then a film crew comes to
Downton to shoot a silent movie.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.901
So there's those two really
great storylines going on.

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:54.868
I didn't get to go to France.
-I know.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.133
I go --
-Thanks for that.

00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:57.367
-Well, you -- when you were
reading the script,

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:58.634
you were like, "Hey, what?
I didn't get to --"

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:00.701
-I kept turning,
and then they returned.

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.501
They came back.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.300
Mary didn't even go out
to join them.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.667
-No.
-Like --

00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:08.300
-But you have -- you have
a really good arc in this film.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:09.634
-But yeah, it's fantastic.
-Either way, it's --

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:12.167
-And it works so great.
The newcomers are brilliant,

00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:14.400
Hugh Darcy, and yeah.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.434
-He's fantastic.
-It was fantastic.

00:05:15.434 --> 00:05:16.801
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:18.934
Here's Michelle Dockery in
"Downton Abbey: A New Era."

00:05:18.934 --> 00:05:20.033
Take a look at this.

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.968
[ Raining ]

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:23.801
-I haven't been up here
for years.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:26.267
-Well, this is the situation
we're in.

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:28.601
With the money, we can bring
the house up to snuff

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:31.033
to match what we've spent
on the estate

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:34.000
and end the 1930s with our heads
held high.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.267
But if you don't want to --
-No.

00:05:38.267 --> 00:05:41.167
No, you steer ahead.

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:42.901
You're the captain now.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:46.734
I am aware of that,
even if you think I'm not.

00:05:46.734 --> 00:05:48.400
-Oh, Michelle Dockery,
everybody.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.634
"Downton Abbey: A New Era"
hits theaters this Friday.

